{"kind":"textbox","id":"sh/1ovahsne","slideIndex":6,"position":{"left":75,"top":610,"width":1110,"height":52},"text":"The PNA estimate can slightly exceed 1 because this correction is approximate. Fidelity itself cannot exceed 1; read this as near ideal.","geometry":"rect","lineWidth":0,"style":{"fontSize":18,"typeface":"Arial","color":"#525252","alignment":"left"}}
{"kind":"chart","id":"ch/v69cryto","slideIndex":6,"position":{"left":75,"top":185,"width":740,"height":410},"name":"","chartType":"line","title":"Ideal-state fidelity by noise strength"}
{"kind":"notes","id":"nt/ra0ovi","slideIndex":6,"text":"The ideal-state fidelity projector is the observable |psi><psi| for the noiseless output. We run PNA on that projector, then evaluate it exactly against the noisy density matrix as a local simulation metric. At 1x noise, raw state fidelity is 0.695 and the full-PNA estimate is 1.005. Because PNA uses a truncated approximate correction, the estimate can exceed the physical maximum of one. Treat it as near ideal, not as a literal fidelity over 1. SLC is applied to the center-X observable workflow on the previous slides; this full projector-fidelity estimate uses full PNA."}
